{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1558 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "9b95bed01c8ef6e08e713a2fe1c6e621173103a68bb4e9d0245b3e121cb1559a" :edb/db-before-t 1557 :edb/db-after-t 1558 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534870 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:18:24.914Z" 13194139534870 17592186045417 true] [17592186117703 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441917 13194139534870 17592186045417 true] [17592186117703 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441917 13194139534870 17592186045417 true] [17592186119543 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 5\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 5I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5\\sin(3t) = 5I + \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5I = 5\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5I_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t) = Ae^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(3t) + \\beta\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(5\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +5\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{15}{34},$ $\\beta = \\dfrac{25}{34}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = -\\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) = - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{15}{34}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{25}{34}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.86\\,\\textrm{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139534870 17592186045417 true] [17592186119543 1074 "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 5\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 5I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5\\sin(3t) = 5I + \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5I = 5\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5I_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t) = Ae^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(3t) + \\beta\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(5\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +5\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{15}{34},$ $\\beta = \\dfrac{25}{34}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = -\\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) = - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = - \\dfrac{15}{34}\\cos(3t) + \\dfrac{25}{34}\\sin(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{15}{34}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{25}{34}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.86\\,\\textrm{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139534870 17592186045417 false] [17592186119962 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441933 13194139534870 17592186045417 true] [17592186119962 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441934 13194139534870 17592186045417 true] [17592186119962 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441935 13194139534870 17592186045417 true] [17592186119964 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441931 13194139534870 17592186045417 true] [17592186119964 1038 #edb/ref 17592186441932 13194139534870 17592186045417 true] [17592186120008 1050 1320.0 13194139534870 17592186045417 true] [17592186120259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534870 17592186045417 true] [17592186120259 1071 "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5V$, a resistance of $20Ω$, and an inductance of $2H$.\nFind, **rounded to two decimal places**, the circuit's current after $0.01$ seconds given that the initial current is $2A$.The current after $0.01$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534870 17592186045417 false] [17592186120259 1071 "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $20\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $2\\,\\text{H}.$\nFind, **rounded to two decimal places**, the circuit's current after $0.01$ seconds given that the initial current is $2\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.01$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes." 13194139534870 17592186045417 true] [17592186120259 1074 "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5=20I+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+10{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+10\\alpha =\\frac{5}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=2,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =2 \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{1}{4} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(7{e}^{-10t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.01$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.01)=\\frac{1}{4}(7{e}^{-10(0.01)}+1)≈1.83\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139534870 17592186045417 true] [17592186120983 1064 "In many cases, the EMF produced by a power source is sinusoidal, not constant. Such a power source is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/43/4398148f91eda2bae1392f79e868e10c8967ecc23ca9f26b791bc1fbacc45ff9.png)\n\nIn a first-order linear RL circuit driven by a sinusoidal voltage, the solution consists of a transient current that decays over time and a steady-state current that oscillates with the same frequency as the input and is independent of the initial condition.\n\nFor example, let's find the amplitude of the steady-state current in the RL circuit shown below.\n\n![](images/59/59fd8a8db1a227a578670d2457011275446c87ba218686f8d1984f4a73a8cfa6.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\sin(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $7\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 7\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 7I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\sin(4t) = 7I + 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 7I = 3\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 7I_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t) = Ae^{-7t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}(4t))+7(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}(4t)) & =3\\operatorname{sin}(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}(4t)+(7\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}(4t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +7\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}(4t)) \\\\ 7\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{12}{65},$ $\\beta = \\dfrac{21}{65}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = -\\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-7t} - \\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-7t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) =  - \\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t) \\approx I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{12}{65}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{21}{65}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.37\\,\\textrm{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. This amplitude represents the maximum magnitude of the steady-state current flowing through the circuit.\n\nWith a sinusoidal EMF, as time increases, the transient current vanishes, leaving a steady-state current that oscillates periodically and is independent of the initial condition.\n\nIn this example, we model the current [math]I(t)[/math] by\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+7I=3\\sin(4t),\n\\]\nwhich has the compelementary solution [math]I_c(t) = Ae^{-7t}[/math] and a particular solution of the form [math]I_p(t)=\\alpha\\cos(\\omega t)+\\beta\\sin(\\omega t).[/math]\n\nHence, the current [math]I(t)[/math] at time [math]t[/math] seconds is given by\n\\[\nI(t) = Ae^{-7t} + \\alpha \\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t)\n\\]\nwhere [math]A,[/math] [math]\\alpha,[/math] and [math]\\beta[/math] are constants." 13194139534870 17592186045417 false] [17592186120983 1064 "In many cases, the EMF produced by a power source is sinusoidal, not constant. Such a power source